<para>(Students were provided access to Desmos with a set \(x\)-axis of \(-10 &lt; x &lt; 50\) and a set \(y\)-axis of \(-60 &lt; y &lt; 350)\).</para>
<para>What is the equation for the curve of best fit that models the data?</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item>\(
    y = -25.41x^2+ 59.28x + 9.64\) </item>
  <item> \(y = -30.84x^2+ 49.61x + 8.99\) </item>
  <item> \(y = -3.72x^2 + 7.34x + 8.67\) </item>
  <item> \(y = -35.78x^2+ 45.21x + 3.74\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = -25.41x^2+ 59.28x + 9.64\)</para>
<para>For questions 5 – 9, use the following information.</para>
<blockquote>The path of a toy rocket after launch is modeled by the following data set. The height of the rocket, in feet, \(y\), corresponds to the number of seconds passed, \(x\).</blockquote>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-bottom="-10" data-left="-2" data-right="7" data-schema-version="1.0" data-tables="[[{&quot;variable&quot;: &quot;x_1&quot;, &quot;values&quot;: [0.4, 1,1.5,2.3,3.5]}, {&quot;variable&quot;: &quot;y_1&quot;, &quot;values&quot;: [20,41,52,60,52]}]]" data-top="70"/>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the equation for the curve of best fit that models the flight of the toy rocket?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item>\(
    y = -6.1x^2+ 31x + 1.24\) </item>
  <item> \(y = -11.9x^2+ 30.2x + 3.64\) </item>
  <item> \(y = -4.2x^2 + 34x + 6.34\) </item>
  <item> \(y = -9.2x^2+ 46x + 3.51\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = -9.2x^2+ 46x + 3.51\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Approximately how high will the rocket be 2 seconds after launch?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 45.91 feet </item>
  <item> 53.60 feet </item>
  <item> 58.71 feet </item>
  <item> 61.00 feet </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 58.71 feet</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>At what approximate times after launch will the rocket have a height of 20 feet?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 0.26 seconds after launch and 4.74 seconds after launch </item>
  <item> 0.39 seconds after launch and 4.61 seconds after launch </item>
  <item> 0.52 seconds after launch and 4.48 seconds after launch </item>
  <item> 0.82 seconds after launch and 4.18 seconds after launch </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 0.39 seconds after launch and 4.61 seconds after launch</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Approximately how long will it take the rocket to reach its maximum height?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 1.73 seconds </item>
  <item> 2.50 seconds </item>
  <item> 3.20 seconds </item>
  <item> 5.08 seconds </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2.50 seconds</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Approximately how long will it take for the rocket to land on the ground?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 2.50 seconds </item>
  <item> 4.63 seconds </item>
  <item> 5.08 seconds </item>
  <item> 6.12 seconds </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5.08 seconds</para>
<para>For question 10, Maria has found a curve of best fit using the technology (Students were provided access to Desmos)</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-bottom="-150" data-equations="[&quot;y=-0.46x^2+14x+17&quot;]" data-left="-10" data-right="35" data-schema-version="1.0" data-top="300"/>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Maria is asked to predict the \(y\)-value on her curve when \(x = 10\). To do this, she graphs the function \(x = 10\) and analyzes the intersection point of the curve and the line.</item>
</list>
<para>What is the \(y\)-value Maria is asked to predict?</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 10 </item>
  <item> 93 </item>
  <item> 105 </item>
  <item> 111 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 111</para></section>
